  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread convection expected through tonight,\nwhile turning cooler and drier into early next week.\n\nThe current setup features a stalled surface boundary still sitting\njust south of the Virginia-North Carolina border as of mid\nafternoon, with a warm front slowly approaching the Appalachians\nfrom the southwest. This is bounded by a modest area of high \npressure drifting offshore of New England. At the same time, a \nshortwave trough is swinging into the upper Ohio Valley. As \nthis trough axis pushes off toward the east, models continue to \nshow a low-level response of enhanced moisture/theta-e \nadvection. With surface to 850-mb winds shifting to mainly south\nto southeasterly, precipitable water values will further rise \nin time. Increasing isentropic lift atop the poleward advancing \nboundary should set the stage for convection through tonight.\n\nThe 12Z high-resolution model suite shows two possible areas of\nmore widespread convective development. (1) As depicted in the \n12Z HREF 40-dBZ paintball plots, supported by regional radar\nimagery as of mid-afternoon, the more pronounced activity forms\nacross West Virginia up into western Pennsylvania. As these \ncells encroach on the Allegheny Front, the slow-moving nature of\nsuch storm elements coupled with saturated soils (per \nsatellite) could afford a flash flood risk in the vicinity of \nwestern MD into the overnight hours. (2) Additionally, the warm\nconveyor belt along the I-95 corridor will likely be an \nadditional focus area for storm genesis, as is currently taking\nplace in southern VA. This may occur more into the evening \nhours locally as this activity is driven by instability \nalong/south of the warm front. How this convection interacts \nwith development coming off the Alleghenies will determine the \nrisk of flash flood potential along the I-95 corridor. High- \nresolution model depictions of this evolution still feature \nquite a bit of spread, but the signal bears watching, especially\ngiven the notably more aggressive 12Z NAM12.\n\nDepending on instability and storm evolution, the mentioned\nconvective complexes could fester into tonight. A preponderance\nof solutions favor shifting this activity into northeastern\nMaryland by around 4-7 AM (early Sunday). Patchy fog could \nmaterialize in the wake, particularly for areas seeing some of \nthe heavier rainfall.\n\nFor Sunday into Monday, multiple temperature swings are\nexpected. Ahead of the approaching cold front, some weak \ndownslope component to the wind fields should steer highs well \ninto the 80s on Sunday. As this front crosses during the \ndiurnal minimum in heating (i.e., overnight Sunday/early \nMonday), storm chances will be rather limited. Will maintain \nshowers and a few rumbles of thunder along the Alleghenies, with\nlower (but not quite zero) probabilities further east across the\npiedmont. Monday's forecast features prevailing northwesterlies\nwith afternoon gusts up to 15 to 25 mph. High temperatures \nshould range from the mid 70s to low 80s, while being locally \ncooler in the mountains. This also comes with falling humidity \nand cool temperatures on Monday night. Robust surface high \npressure crossing the northeastern U.S. will support widespread \n50s overnight. Even some upper 40s are likely in spots that can\nfully decouple and remain cloud-free.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm and dry ahead of a mid-week cold\nfront.\n\nHigh pressure will continue to build on Tuesday as mid-level dry air \nmoves in from the Great Lakes. Expect seasonable and dry \nconditions to persist during this time as highs reach into the \nlower 80s with dewpoints potentially dipping into the upper 40s\non Tuesday morning.\n\nStrong upper-level ridging builds toward the area Tuesday into\nWednesday, then begins to break down by late in the week as a \nretrograding low tracks south into the Florida Peninsula from \nthe Atlantic. Humidity likely begins to increase again on \nWednesday as any lingering dry air is driven east out of the \nregion. Meanwhile, a trough will begin to develop over the \nNorthern Plains with a stemming cold front tracking east through\nthe Mid-Atlantic sometime in the mid-to-late week period. \nUpdated model guidance has delayed the arrival of this front \npotentially to Thursday & into Friday, with showers & \npotentially a few thunderstorms accompanying the front. However,\nsignificant uncertainty remains in the time of arrival here as \nmodels continue to grasp this synoptic setup. Regardless, \nsevere probabilities continue to look low as we begin to \ntransition out of the summertime thunderstorm season.",
